# OnionPress container image pins — THE canonical source of truth.
#
# Generated by build/refresh-image-digests.sh. Do not hand-edit a digest: the
# script writes this file first, then propagates the literals into every
# consumer so a bare `docker compose up` with no environment still works.
#
# Consumers (all kept in sync by that script, all enforced by
# tests/test_image_pins.py):
#   app/Resources/docker/docker-compose.yml   tor, wordpress, onionheaven
#                                             (+ its ONIONHEAVEN_IMAGE env),
#                                             db, autoheal, autoheal-proxy
#   app/Resources/docker/docker-compose.cloudflare.yml   cloudflared
#   app/MacOS/onionpress                      KEY_HELPER_IMAGE (one-shot key helpers)
#   linux/onionpress                          KEY_HELPER_IMAGE (one-shot key helpers)
#   src/onionpress/containers.py              ONIONHEAVEN_IMAGE
#   src/onionpress/launcher_ops.py            DEFAULT_TOR_IMAGE
#
# The two ONIONPRESS_* images are built by this project. Every consumer reads
# those names as an override with the pin as default, so exporting either
# variable points the whole stack at a different image — that is how
# build/build-images.sh hands you a locally built stack.
#
# The other four are third-party images the stack runs as published, mirrored
# under our own ghcr.io namespace (build/image-mirrors.txt; the copy is made by
# .github/workflows/mirror-images.yml, issue #501), so a publisher deleting an
# old digest cannot stop a first run. A pin carries the upstream's digest.
# They are plain literals in the consumers (no override: nobody builds them locally,
# and using_local_images() would not gate a knob it does not know about).
# Bump one by giving the script a new UPSTREAM tag, e.g.
#   build/refresh-image-digests.sh --mariadb mariadb:13.0.3
# which resolves it with build/base-image-digest.sh (no daemon needed). A plain
# refresh re-resolves every third-party TAG below: Docker Hub rebuilds a
# version tag for base-OS patches, and that is how those reach a release.
#
# mariadb: pin forward only. The data directory follows whatever version has
# run on it (MARIADB_AUTO_UPGRADE=1 in the compose file), so a pin older than
# what an install already runs would refuse to start. Until this file pinned
# it, every launch pulled mariadb:latest, so a new pin must never be older
# than what :latest was at the previous release.
#
# Why digests and not tags: `:latest` is mutable, and so is `13.0.2`. A digest
# is the only thing that makes "the v2.4.110 tor image" mean one specific set
# of bytes on both amd64 and arm64. These MUST be multi-arch index digests
# (what `docker buildx imagetools inspect <tag>` and
# build/base-image-digest.sh report), never a single-platform manifest
# digest, or the pin resolves on one architecture and 404s on the other.

ONIONPRESS_TOR_IMAGE=ghcr.io/brewsterkahle/onionpress-tor:latest@sha256:eea0b54a04ebe60a0db66ebcff2d5cf1875485cadcee1df3ce80e36374a039dc
ONIONPRESS_WORDPRESS_IMAGE=ghcr.io/brewsterkahle/onionpress-wordpress:latest@sha256:ef35899bf2dbed58f7a64ece2d3008e5e428b7a9ac5f4ddfcb363d8e8bce57a0
MARIADB_IMAGE=ghcr.io/brewsterkahle/onionpress-mariadb:13.0.2@sha256:4844586367ef1b639ae699953e00a6a18f460f700e52df00401e1101a6ef2450
AUTOHEAL_IMAGE=ghcr.io/brewsterkahle/onionpress-autoheal:latest@sha256:df19e62e64c515d914a67d7ad4e45351e31cb44482de60c58c8bfa1181bd0b03
CLOUDFLARED_IMAGE=ghcr.io/brewsterkahle/onionpress-cloudflared:2026.10.0@sha256:9b49eed8f62806d5d45ddf59ecefb5710429598ea6d3fcccd2af938f621b2b07
SOCKET_PROXY_IMAGE=ghcr.io/brewsterkahle/onionpress-socket-proxy:1.13.1@sha256:e78e68ba3ea4a6bf8ee47865ac1d1f98d525c3660df948554e766283c40d3f4b
